dict(source="Pick",title="How Squid Game Turned Rage and Desperation Into a Radical Hit",author="R.O. Kwon (Vanity Fair)",url="https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2022/05/squid-game-season-2-awards-insider",minutes=14,tags=["story","craft","microdrama","culture"],ideas=["In 2009 Hwang Dong-hyuk, in debt along with his mother and grandmother, wrote Squid Game as a film. Investors called it too unrealistic and too violent, so it sat in a drawer for ten years.","By 2018 rising inequality made the premise feel real, and Netflix, newly building in Asia, turned it into a nine-episode series. Within ten days it was the most watched Netflix show in 90 countries.","Hwang built it on gambling because desperate people drop their masks and show 'very pure emotions'. He kept rewriting scenes the night before shooting, such as the dying Sang-woo reaching out a hand to Gi-hun, to find a glimpse of humanity in a villain."],q="Which of Thriving's shelved or rejected story ideas might the moment have caught up with, and what is the one raw emotion your next series is built on?",added="2026-10-28",score=9,featured=True),
dict(source="Newsletter",title="Databricks & the Future of the Firm",author="a16z",url="https://www.a16z.news/p/databricks-and-the-future-of-the",minutes=28,tags=["AI","business","founders","strategy"],ideas=["Databricks has passed a $7 billion revenue run rate, growing 80% a year. It now sees more queries from agents than from people, and agents launch more than 17 million databases on it every day.","The piece argues agents need a shared system of record (what is true, what data means, who can do what, what worked) so people and agents can act without routing every decision through layers of managers.","The long-run change isn't 'everyone is an engineer' but 'everyone is a manager': agents handle work that's already figured out, and people judge what's good and decide what should exist next."],q="If your agents and editors all worked from one shared record of what each client wants and what passed QC, which decisions could stop waiting on you?",added="2026-10-28",score=7),
dict(source="Newsletter",title="Let Chaos Reign, and Then Rein In Chaos",author="a16z (Replit essay)",url="https://www.a16z.news/p/let-chaos-reign-and-then-rein-in",minutes=8,tags=["AI","business","strategy"],ideas=["Written for the launch of Replit for Business: most AI agents today live in chaos, in chat windows, personal accounts and one-off scripts, and nobody can see what they did.","PCs, spreadsheets and email all entered offices the same messy way, with people lying on purchase forms to get PCs and 'rogue spreadsheets' that only their maker understood. The winners built a habitat instead of a ban.","The goal is 'free-range' agents: not caged, not running wild, but inside rules for access, audit and versioning that keep the bottom-up energy alive."],q="Which of your team's personal AI hacks should become a shared, logged studio workflow, and which should stay free-range?",added="2026-10-28",score=6),
dict(source="X",title="Give Claude 17M+ free films and 14M audio recordings for video edits",author="@ElliotPadfield",url="https://x.com/ElliotPadfield/status/2113393903923581149",minutes=2,tags=["AI","video","tools"],ideas=["The Internet Archive has a free API that needs no key, documented at archive.org/developers.","It holds 10,000+ public-domain Prelinger films for stock and archival b-roll, 187,000 78rpm records and 300,000+ live concert recordings.","His suggested standing instruction to Claude: whenever you need archival b-roll or period music, search the Prelinger and 78rpm collections and use them in the edit."],q="Could Almanac reels or period microdramas pull free archival b-roll and music instead of generating every shot, and what licence check would you need first?",added="2026-10-28",score=5),
